WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.350
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.080
Today we are going to travel in time.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.400
With the ascendancy of neural-network based
learning techniques, this previous method

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:17.890
enables us to take and old old black and white
movie that suffers from a lot of problems

00:00:17.890 --> 00:00:24.480
like missing data, flickering, and more, give
it to a neural network, and have it restore

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:25.650
it for us.

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:31.780
And here, you can not only see how much better
this restored version is, but, it did take

00:00:31.780 --> 00:00:34.000
it one step further.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.149
It also performed colorization!

00:00:36.149 --> 00:00:42.890
Essentially, here, we could produce 6 colorized
reference images, and the neural network uses

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:49.399
them as art direction and propagates all this
information to the remainder of the frames.

00:00:49.399 --> 00:00:54.859
So this work did restoration and colorization
at the same time.

00:00:54.859 --> 00:01:00.350
This was absolutely amazing, and now comes
something even better, today, we have a new

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:07.130
piece of work that performs not only restoration
and colorization, but super-resolution as

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:08.130
well!

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:13.340
What this means is that we can take an antique
photo, which suffers from a lot of issues.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:20.020
Look, these old films exaggerate wrinkles
a great deal, they even even darken the lips

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.660
and do funny things with red colors.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:28.860
For instance, subsurface scattering is also
missing, this is light penetrating our skin

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:34.050
and bouncing inside before coming out again,
and the lack of this effect is why the skin

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:36.490
looks a little plasticky here.

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:40.200
Luckily, we can simulate all these phenomena
on our computers.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:45.660
I am a light transport researcher by trade,
and this is from our earlier paper with the

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:51.250
Activision Blizzard game development company,
this is the same phenomenon, a simulation

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:56.940
without subsurface scattering, and this one
is with simulating this effect.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.340
Beautiful.

00:01:58.340 --> 00:02:01.460
You can find a link to this paper in the video
description.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:07.280
So with all these problems with the antique
photos, our question is, what did Lincoln

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.550
really look like?

00:02:08.550 --> 00:02:16.040
Well, let’s try an earlier framework for
restoration, colorization and super resolution…and.

00:02:16.040 --> 00:02:20.870
Well, unfortunately, most of our issues still
remain.

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:26.599
Lots of exaggerated wrinkles, plasticky look,
lots of detail is missing.

00:02:26.599 --> 00:02:28.469
Can we do better?

00:02:28.469 --> 00:02:34.530
Well, hold on to your papers, and observe
the output with the new technique.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.409
Wow.

00:02:36.409 --> 00:02:42.010
The restoration indeed took place properly,
brought the wrinkles down to a much more realistic

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:48.269
level, skin looks like skin because of subsurface
scattering, and the super resolution part

00:02:48.269 --> 00:02:54.739
is responsible for a lot of new detail everywhere,
but especially around the lips.

00:02:54.739 --> 00:02:56.079
Outstanding.

00:02:56.079 --> 00:03:01.099
It truly feels like this photo has been rephotographed
with a modern camera.

00:03:01.099 --> 00:03:05.109
And with that, please meet Time-Travel Rephotography.

00:03:05.109 --> 00:03:10.140
And the curious thing is that all this sounds
flat out impossible.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.140
Why is that?

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:16.499
Since we don’t have old and new image pairs
of Lincoln and many other historic figures,

00:03:16.499 --> 00:03:22.310
the question naturally arises in the mind
of the curious Fellow Scholar - how do we

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:25.650
train a neural network to perform this?

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:29.560
And the answer is that we need to use their
siblings.

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:34.079
Now this doesn’t mean that Lincoln had a
long lost sibling that we don’t know about.

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:39.340
What this means is that as the input image
is fed through our neural network, we can

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:46.340
generate a photorealistic image of someone,
and this someone kind of resembles the target

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:49.389
subject, and has all the details filled in.

00:03:49.389 --> 00:03:55.959
Then, in the next step, we can start morphing
the sibling until is starts resembling the

00:03:55.959 --> 00:03:57.670
test subject.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:04.230
With this previously existing StyleGAN2 technique,
morphing is now easy to do, but restoration

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:09.700
is hard, so essentially, with this, we can
skip the difficult restoration part, and just

00:04:09.700 --> 00:04:12.689
do the easier morphing instead.

00:04:12.689 --> 00:04:16.789
Trading a difficult problem for an easier
one.

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:18.739
Absolutely brilliant idea.

00:04:18.739 --> 00:04:24.389
And if you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now, squeeze that paper, because it

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:26.900
can do even more.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:28.120
Age progression!

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:29.150
Look.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:34.530
If we have only a few a few target photos
of Thomas Edison throughout his life, these

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:39.940
will be our yardsticks, and the algorithm
is able to generate his aging process between

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:41.960
these yardstick images.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:47.060
And the best part is that these images have
different lighting, pose, and none of this

00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:48.600
is an issue for the technique.

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.110
It just doesn’t care and it still works
beautifully.

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:54.250
Wow.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:05:00.389
So we saw earlier that there are other methods
that attempt to do this too, at least the

00:05:00.389 --> 00:05:01.979
colorization part.

00:05:01.979 --> 00:05:06.020
Yes, we have colorization and other techniques
in abundance.

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:08.699
So how does this compare to them?

00:05:08.699 --> 00:05:12.070
It appears to outpace all of them really convincingly.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:19.159
The numbers from the user study and the algorithmically
generated scores also favor the new technique.

00:05:19.159 --> 00:05:20.849
This is a huge leap forward.

00:05:20.849 --> 00:05:24.910
Do you have some other applications in mind
for this new technique?

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:29.069
Let me know in the comments what you would
do with this or how you would like to see

00:05:29.069 --> 00:05:31.139
it improved.

00:05:31.139 --> 00:05:35.030
Now, of course, not even this technique is
perfect.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:38.979
Blurry and noisy regions can still appear
here and there.

00:05:38.979 --> 00:05:44.569
And note that StyleGAN2, the basis for this
algorithm came out just a little more than

00:05:44.569 --> 00:05:45.980
a year ago.

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:51.940
And it is amazing that we are witnessing such
incredible progress in so little time.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.500
My goodness.

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:59.540
And just imagine what the next paper down
the line will bring!

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:31.750
What a 
time 

00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:45.880
to be alive!

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:49.860
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

